onekeyirc 0.1.33 - build and installation
========================================

This file covers installation and maintenance. Start with README.md for
normal use, MANUAL.txt for the full reference, SECURITY.md for operational
limits, and doc/OPENBSD-VPS.txt for unattended OpenBSD startup.

REQUIREMENTS

    C11 compiler, standard C library, POSIX shell, and make
    POSIX terminal, poll, socket, fork, and descriptor interfaces
    Descriptor-relative filesystem operations (openat/fstatat/mkdirat)
        and no-follow opens, plus a monotonic clock
    GNU/BSD-style getopt_long option parsing
    OpenSSL-compatible TLS development headers and libraries
    A maintained certificate authority trust store for public TLS servers
    An ANSI-compatible terminal for interactive operation

onekeyirc has no curses or terminfo dependency. Its interface uses
read/write, termios, poll, and ANSI terminal sequences directly.

The TLS code requires OpenSSL 1.1.1-compatible APIs or newer. LibreSSL is
usable when the installed headers and libraries provide those APIs; use
matching development files and libraries maintained by your operating
system. Configure checks compilation and linking, not a real TLS account
or every platform behavior. API compatibility is not a security-update
policy: use a vendor-maintained TLS library with current security fixes.
The review environment used OpenSSL 3.5.7; its later certificate-processing
fix for CVE-2026-35189 is in 3.5.9 or the vendor equivalent. Deployment
libraries and backports must be checked separately; see SECURITY.md.

The installed client does not require Python or Bash. Optional encrypted
credentials invoke gpg from GnuPG 2.2.20 or newer in PATH and require an agent
able to decrypt without prompting. No GPG library is linked. Private-file
credentials and ordinary IRC do not require GnuPG.

BUILD

From the extracted source directory:

    ./configure
    make
    ./onekeyirc --version
    ./onekeyirc --help

Configure does not download dependencies or install files. It writes a
host-specific Makefile and config.log. Reconfigure on the actual target OS;
do not copy a generated Makefile or executable from another platform.
Makefile.in is the distributed template.

The configure command accepts --prefix=DIR. Supply build variables through
the environment, before ./configure:

    CC=cc CFLAGS='-O2 -g' ./configure
    make

For headers and libraries in an extra prefix:

    CPPFLAGS=-I/usr/local/include LDFLAGS=-L/usr/local/lib ./configure

For explicit TLS linkage:

    TLS_LIBS='-lssl -lcrypto' ./configure

CC, CPPFLAGS, CFLAGS, LDFLAGS, and TLS_LIBS are supported.
Configure probes and adds supported compiler/linker hardening options,
including stack protection, format diagnostics, PIE, and applicable ELF
linker protections. Available flags vary by toolchain and OS; inspect
config.log rather than assuming a particular protection was enabled.

The source targets Unix-like systems, including Linux and BSD. Historical
platform reports are in doc/OPENBSD-VALIDATION.txt and the dated validation
files. They state what was actually run. This documentation release does
not turn a previous Linux result into an OpenBSD, LibreSSL, or real-bouncer
compatibility test.

Resolver helpers close unrelated inherited descriptors before lookup.
Supported facilities include close_range, closefrom, or a complete procfs/
devfs descriptor scan. If the client cannot completely isolate a helper,
connection setup reports a failure. Do not remove the check to make an
unsupported environment appear compatible.

LOCAL CHECKS

    make check-unit
    make check

check-unit runs the C tests; make check also exercises local integration
paths. Integration tests need Python 3, PTYs, and local sockets; some checks
use Bash job control or the openssl command to create local TLS fixtures.
These are development dependencies, not installed-client dependencies.
Tests use temporary files and simulated/local peers rather than your real
IRC account. A restricted runner that denies PTYs or local sockets cannot
complete the corresponding tests; record that limitation separately.

For a narrower change, use the appropriate existing target:

    make check-security
    make check-security-boundaries
    make check-bouncers
    make check-session-controls
    make check-session-lifecycle
    make check-idle-display
    make check-efficiency
    make check-terminal-signals

Optional GPG checks need GnuPG:

    make check-gpg
    make check-setup-gpg

For a compiler with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer:

    make sanitize
    make check-security-sanitize

Instrumented test binaries are separate from the ordinary client. Consult
the target recipe and test report before interpreting a skipped or failed
sanitizer run; a restricted runtime may not support every sanitizer facility.

Documentation maintainers additionally need Python 3:

    make docs
    make check-docs

The checked-in manual exports are generated from the in-program help. Keep
them synchronized using these targets when help changes. Ordinary end-user
compilation and installation use the supplied exports and do not require
Python. See DESIGN.md for the documentation maintenance workflow.

TRY WITHOUT INSTALLING

    ./onekeyirc --test-mode
    ./onekeyirc --manual

Test mode disables IRC/DCC network activity, credential-file reads and
decryption, config saves, and command-count persistence. Explicit session
backgrounding can still create local session IPC. It is useful for
inspecting the UI, not for validating your remote server or password.

Normal interactive operation requires a terminal. --help, --version,
--manual, and --check-config work without one. See README.md for first
connection, saved configuration, and busy-session recovery. In a shell,
quote channel arguments beginning with # so they are not parsed as comments:

    ./onekeyirc --server irc.example.org --channel '#mychannel' --nick mynick

INSTALL

The default prefix is /usr/local. For a per-user installation:

    ./configure --prefix="$HOME/.local"
    make
    make install

Ensure that prefix's bin directory is in your PATH. To install system-wide,
configure the intended prefix first and use the privilege mechanism supplied
by your OS. On OpenBSD, for example:

    ./configure --prefix=/usr/local
    make
    doas make install

The install layout is:

    PREFIX/bin/onekeyirc
    PREFIX/share/man/man1/onekeyirc.1
    PREFIX/share/doc/onekeyirc/

The source archive includes no precompiled OpenBSD executable. Installation
does not create user configuration, connect to IRC, enable a service, or
replace an already running worker's in-memory executable.

For packaging, stage under an absolute DESTDIR while retaining the desired
final prefix:

    make install DESTDIR=/absolute/staging/root

DESTDIR is prepended to installed paths; it is not the runtime prefix.
Use the same configured paths for install and uninstall.

OPENBSD SERVICE WRAPPERS

    doas make install-openbsd-service

This optional target installs the executable, documentation, and
the onekeyirc wrapper. The plural target name
install-openbsd-services is an alias. It requires OpenBSD, root, and the
bundled /usr/local/bin layout; it refuses a symlink/non-directory rc.d
destination. It does not enable or start the service, create its account,
or edit rc.conf settings.

The compiled program belongs in /usr/local/bin. The installed /etc/rc.d
file is the supplied text Korn shell wrapper, not a renamed executable.
Choose an existing non-root runtime account and an absolute config path
before starting it. Follow doc/OPENBSD-VPS.txt in order, including the
interactive and unattended tests before boot enablement.

UPGRADE AND REMOVE

Back up the primary config, its .servers catalog and .d profiles, and any
separate credential files and optional logs using private permissions.
Read CHANGELOG.md and SECURITY.md for changes relevant to your deployment.
Reconfigure and build the new source rather than reusing old build outputs.

make install first copies and sets permissions on a temporary executable in
its destination directory, then renames that complete file into place. This
supports replacing an executable while an older worker is running. A failed
binary staging step preserves the previous executable; a failed fresh staging
step publishes no partial binary. A directory at the executable path is
refused. The destination directory and build tree must be trusted. Man pages
and documentation install separately: the whole install is not a transaction.

A live worker keeps running the old code until it exits. Inspect --sessions,
save wanted settings, and deliberately close or quit that job before starting
the replacement. Closing ends all IRC connections and transfers and loses
its RAM-only drafts/history. A new process is not a resumed old job. If an
OpenBSD service owns it, stop that service with its existing wrapper before
replacing the wrapper, then follow the service guide to restart it.

If upgrading from the standalone name 1keyirc, read
doc/ONEKEYIRC-RENAME.txt before replacing executables or service scripts.
Stop the old service using its old wrapper first. Legacy configuration is
reused in place only when the new default path is absent; no migration is
performed automatically. vipirc configurations are independent.

    make uninstall

Run uninstall with the same prefix, DESTDIR, and privileges used to install.
It removes the installed executable, man page, and installed documentation.
It does not stop a running worker or delete your configurations, credentials,
logs, or separately installed rc.d wrappers. Stop/disable a service before
removing its executable; service management remains explicit.

    make clean
    make distclean

clean removes build outputs. distclean also removes the generated Makefile
and config.log so the next build starts with ./configure.

SECURITY REGRESSIONS

    make check-security-pass
    make check-security-pass-sanitize
    make check-hotkeys-terminal

These targets cover config creation races, replayed authentication
privacy, deterministic DCC parser mutations, and terminal paste/hotkey
state. Full vipirc also runs its curses UI fixture; it needs native
wide-curses development files. Sanitizer checks need compiler/runtime
support. Existing check-security targets remain broader. Read the
current review for executed workloads and unavailable checks.

RECOVERY REGRESSIONS

    make check-stability
    make check-stability-sanitize
    make check-install
    make check-idle-load

check-stability covers interrupted LIST requests, short/failed terminal writes,
and credential-helper resource failures and retry. Its sanitizer variant needs
ASan/UBSan support. check-install uses the actual install recipe in private
staging directories, including replacement of a running test executable and
injected copy/permission/rename failures; it does not install the client.
check-idle-load runs a bounded 60-second load against eight private TLS peers
through the minimalist terminal. It needs Python 3, openssl, loopback sockets
and a PTY; it does not contact public IRC. This is not a long-running soak.
The current review records what was run and any platform restrictions.

BUILD TROUBLESHOOTING

- Missing TLS headers or unresolved SSL symbols: inspect config.log and
  supply matching headers/libraries through CPPFLAGS, LDFLAGS, and TLS_LIBS.
  Certificate-verification settings cannot fix a compile/link failure.
- Wrong executable after an upgrade: inspect your shell's command path and
  onekeyirc --version, then check whether you resumed an old worker.
- A configure result from another OS: regenerate it on the destination.
- Tests fail before creating a PTY or local socket: report the runner's
  restriction and the uncompleted test; do not call it a passing check.
- Authentication fails only under a service: compare Unix user, absolute
  config/credential paths, permissions, and the GPG agent environment.

Use the README troubleshooting section and the in-program /help for runtime
questions. Security-sensitive reports should follow SECURITY.md.

Custom key bindings use a new [hotkeys] config section (see doc/HOTKEYS.txt).
Older releases reject this section, even when empty. Before downgrading,
restore a compatible backup or remove that whole section and its rows.
Validate using the older binary before starting it.
